WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.397
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:09.164
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:16.343
Unit six: Participle Clauses. Today you will join ideas concisely with present and past participles.

4
00:00:16.643 --> 00:00:19.098
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.417
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:25.447
Having finished.

7
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:30.330
Having finished means a perfect participle that shows an earlier action.

8
00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:32.870
Having finished, she left.

9
00:00:33.483 --> 00:00:36.577
Your turn. Say: Having finished.

10
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:40.585
Having finished.

11
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.757
Walking down the street.

12
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:48.246
Walking down the street means a present participle showing a simultaneous action.

13
00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.826
Walking down the street, I saw him.

14
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.452
Your turn. Say: Walking down the street.

15
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.928
Walking down the street.

16
00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:01.021
Present participle.

17
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:05.096
Present participle means the -ing form used in a clause.

18
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:07.857
Smiling is a present participle.

19
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:11.451
Your turn. Say: Present participle.

20
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.661
Present participle.

21
00:01:16.417 --> 00:01:17.801
Past participle.

22
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:22.546
Past participle means the -ed form with a passive meaning.

23
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.417
Built in 1900, it is old.

24
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:28.843
Your turn. Say: Past participle.

25
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.154
Past participle.

26
00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:35.382
Reduce a clause.

27
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:39.590
Reduce a clause means to shorten a clause with a participle.

28
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:42.264
Reduce the clause to save words.

29
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.094
Your turn. Say: Reduce a clause.

30
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:50.387
Reduce a clause.

31
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.198
Concise.

32
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.078
Concise means short but clear.

33
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:57.300
A concise sentence.

34
00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.466
Your turn. Say: Concise.

35
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.382
Concise.

36
00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:06.245
Formal writing.

37
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.411
Formal writing means a serious style used in essays.

38
00:02:11.017 --> 00:02:13.829
Participle clauses suit formal writing.

39
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:17.568
Your turn. Say: Formal writing.

40
00:02:20.433 --> 00:02:21.551
Formal writing.

41
00:02:22.317 --> 00:02:23.829
Connect ideas.

42
00:02:24.817 --> 00:02:27.652
Connect ideas means to join thoughts logically.

43
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:29.948
Connect ideas smoothly.

44
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.168
Your turn. Say: Connect ideas.

45
00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.441
Connect ideas.

46
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:42.128
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.983 --> 00:02:47.273
Visal, how can I combine: She finished her work. She went home?

48
00:02:48.383 --> 00:03:00.767
Having finished her work, she went home. The perfect participle shows that the first action was complete before the second. Both clauses must share the same subject.

49
00:03:01.883 --> 00:03:04.173
What about simultaneous actions?

50
00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:20.071
Use the present participle: Walking down the street, I met an old friend. It means While I was walking. Past participles are passive: Built in 1900, the school is still in use.

51
00:03:21.183 --> 00:03:22.486
What is the danger?

52
00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:35.608
The dangling participle. Walking down the street, the rain started is wrong, because the rain did not walk. The subject of the main clause must perform the participle action.

53
00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:38.058
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:46.001
Grammar focus: Participle clause: participle plus object and adverbial, plus main clause (same subject).

55
00:03:46.301 --> 00:03:47.407
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.117 --> 00:03:50.302
Having finished her work, she went home.

57
00:03:51.217 --> 00:03:54.133
Walking down the street, I met an old friend.

58
00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:58.338
Built in 1900, the school is still in use.

59
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:07.455
Use -ing for active simultaneous actions, having + participle for an earlier action, and -ed for passive meaning. Keep the same subject.

60
00:04:08.217 --> 00:04:12.076
Be careful! Don't say: Walking home, the rain started.

61
00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:16.370
Say: Walking home, I got wet.

62
00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:18.565
Avoid a dangling participle.

63
00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:22.298
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.670
Saying More with Less.

65
00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.991
Having finished the exam, the students left the hall.

66
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.777
Walking down the street, I met an old friend.

67
00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:36.113
Built in 1900, the school is still in use.

68
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.144
Participle clauses make writing shorter and more elegant.

69
00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:44.120
But the subject of both clauses must be the same.

70
00:04:45.233 --> 00:04:49.235
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:50.083 --> 00:04:52.048
blank the exam, she went home.

72
00:04:52.348 --> 00:04:56.652
A, Finish. B, Having finished. C, Finished having.

73
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.699
The answer is B: Having finished.

74
00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:06.186
Which is a present participle clause?

75
00:05:06.486 --> 00:05:12.891
A, Walking home, I saw him.. B, Built in 1900, it stands.. C, To walk home, I left..

76
00:05:16.433 --> 00:05:19.013
The answer is A: Walking home, I saw him..

77
00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.672
Which sentence has a dangling participle?

78
00:05:22.972 --> 00:05:29.064
A, Walking home, I saw him.. B, Walking home, the rain started.. C, Having eaten, we left..

79
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.586
The answer is B: Walking home, the rain started..

80
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.655
Built in 1900 has a blank meaning.

81
00:05:39.955 --> 00:05:44.352
A, passive. B, negative. C, future.

82
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.329
The answer is A: passive.

83
00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:54.133
According to the reading, when was the school built?

84
00:05:54.433 --> 00:06:00.211
A, 1900. B, 1990. C, 1800.

85
00:06:03.767 --> 00:06:06.138
The answer is A: 1900.

86
00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:10.682
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:11.517 --> 00:06:14.979
Combine two sentences into one using a participle clause.

88
00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:25.400
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:38.261
I opened the door. I saw a surprise. Opening the door, I saw a surprise. The participle clause makes the sentence shorter, and the subject I is the same in both parts.

90
00:06:38.561 --> 00:06:40.305
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:41.217 --> 00:06:49.964
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and participle clause: participle plus object and adverbial, plus main clause (same subject).

92
00:06:50.264 --> 00:06:55.657
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:06:55.957 --> 00:06:58.551
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:01.016
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

